        "extraction_error_parts": {},
        "assets": [],
        "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14199233/observations/lesson-step-32691-abde34c158f2f1fe98e7.html",
        "screenshot": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14199233/observations/lesson-step-32691-abde34c158f2f1fe98e7.png",
        "source_url": "https://mathacademy.com/topics/1362",
        "captured_at": 1791972431.6261976,
        "math_academy_id": 5207,
        "topic_id": "1362",
        "sequence_position": null,
        "source_step": {
          "dom_id": "step-t5207",
          "placement_id": null,
          "content_id": null,
          "type": null
        },
        "content": "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nSince the hyperbolic functions are defined in terms of exponential functions, we can easily calculate their derivatives. \n\nThe derivatives of the basic hyperbolic functions are as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x) & =\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x) & =\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x) & ={sech}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's use these results to find the derivative of the following function:\n\n$$\ny = \\sinh{x} - 2\\cosh{x}\n$$\n\nUsing the usual properties of derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x)-2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        "title": "Introduction"
      },
      {
        "dom_id": "step-32711",
        "name": "Deriving the Formula for the Derivative of the Hyperbolic Sine",
        "problem": "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n%$\n\nWe would like the prove the following result:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(\\sinh{x}) = \\cosh x\n$$\n\nTo do this, we revert to the definition of the hyperbolic sine:\n\n$$\n\\sinh x = \\dfrac{1}{2}(e^x-e^{-x}).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equation with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe proof for the derivative of the hyperbolic cosine is very similar. You may wish to try it for yourself.",
        "worked_solution": "",
        "calculator_instructions": "",
        "fields": [],
        "problem_field_keys": [],
        "answer_fields_complete": null,
