        "assets": [],
        "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14199233/observations/lesson-step-32711-66dd0c0a832c7ec5e36d.html",
        "screenshot": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14199233/observations/lesson-step-32711-66dd0c0a832c7ec5e36d.png",
        "source_url": "https://mathacademy.com/topics/1362",
        "captured_at": 1791972435.11198,
        "math_academy_id": 5221,
        "topic_id": "1362",
        "sequence_position": null,
        "source_step": {
          "dom_id": "step-t5221",
          "placement_id": null,
          "content_id": null,
          "type": null
        },
        "content": "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n%$\n\nWe would like the prove the following result:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(\\sinh{x}) = \\cosh x\n$$\n\nTo do this, we revert to the definition of the hyperbolic sine:\n\n$$\n\\sinh x = \\dfrac{1}{2}(e^x-e^{-x}).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equation with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe proof for the derivative of the hyperbolic cosine is very similar. You may wish to try it for yourself.",
        "title": "Deriving the Formula for the Derivative of the Hyperbolic Sine"
      },
      {
        "dom_id": "step-32713",
        "name": "Deriving the Formula for the Derivative of the Hyperbolic Tangent",
        "problem": "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nWe would like the prove the following result:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}(\\tanh{x}) = \\sech^2{x}\n$$\n\nWe do this using the definition of the hyperbolic tangent. The most convenient definition to work with for this purpose is\n\n$$\n\\tanh{x} = \\dfrac{e^{2x}-1}{e^{2x}+1}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equation with respect to $x$ using the quotient rule, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{e}^{2x}-1}{{e}^{2x}+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{({e}^{2x}-1)^{′}\\cdot ({e}^{2x}+1)-({e}^{2x}-1)\\cdot ({e}^{2x}+1)^{′}}{({e}^{2x}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2{e}^{2x})({e}^{2x}+1)-({e}^{2x}-1)(2{e}^{2x})}{({e}^{2x}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{4x}+2{e}^{2x}-2{e}^{4x}+2{e}^{2x}}{({e}^{2x}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{e}^{2x}}{({e}^{2x}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{2{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1})}^{2} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{2}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}})}^{2} \\\\  & ={sech}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        "worked_solution": "",
        "calculator_instructions": "",
