      {
        "key": "step-q95683",
        "question": {
          "dom_id": "step-q95683",
          "name": "Question 7",
          "problem": "$$\n\nFind the derivative of $f(x)=\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}.$",
          "worked_solution": "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf=\\frac{2}{g},\\,g=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=-\\frac{2}{{g}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x,\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two results together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2}{{g}^{2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sinh}⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sinh}⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
          "calculator_instructions": "",
